◆ What does it apply to?
Which kind of work does this rule cover?

Arturo can spot these in a task you type

1. human-contact — Prepare or route work that may need a Human participant.
2. safety — Identify safety risks, constraints, and missing evidence in supplied material.
3. backup — Represent eligibility to create and verify backups through an approved backup mechanism.
4. web-research — Search an approved provider and capture normalized source results as an exact, dated evidence Artefact before model analysis.
5. writing — Draft and structure written content from supplied context.
6. research — Investigate a question and separate evidence, assumptions, and uncertainty.
7. review — Evaluate supplied work for quality, completeness, accuracy, and risk.
8. analysis — Organize supplied information into comparisons, patterns, and conclusions.
9. conversion — Transform content between formats while preserving its intended meaning.
10. advice — Offer practical recommendations with trade-offs and risks.
11. planning — Break an objective into ordered, achievable steps and dependencies.
12. coordination — Route work and organize handoffs between suitable Workers or ongoing Functions.
13. monitoring — Observe supplied state and report significant changes or exceptions.
14. simulation — Explore possible outcomes using explicit assumptions.
15. optimization — Compare alternatives and improve toward a stated objective.
16. negotiation — Prepare positions, options, and trade-offs for a negotiation.
17. coaching — Give supportive, practical guidance intended to improve performance over time.
18. email-draft — Draft email content for review.
19. summarisation — Condense supplied content while preserving its important meaning and limits.

It will not spot these in a task you type. A rule on one of them still
applies to work that declares it another way — a Procedure, a Workflow
step, or a task a running Function creates from an Event:

20. human-review — Take part in an accountable Human review or approval step.
21. execution — Carry out a precisely defined step through a runtime-approved mechanism.
22. email-send — Allowed to send email, once email has been set up and approved.
23. file-read — Represent eligibility to read files through a runtime-approved tool.
24. file-write — Represent eligibility to create or update files through a runtime-approved tool.
25. shell-read — Represent eligibility for non-mutating shell inspection through an approved tool.
26. shell-execute — Allowed to run commands, through an approved tool only.
27. web — Represent eligibility to use a general approved web or connector integration.
28. llm — General language-model reasoning and response generation.

